1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,400
 Android is an open source operating system which is widely used in mobile devices.

2
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:17,680
 It is also used in automotive devices, elevations, via devices, etc.

3
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:23,160
 It has also a corresponding open source project which is led by Google.

4
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:28,520
 On the right side you can see a high level architecture diagram of AOSP.

5
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:35,400
 So as you can see, Android has multiple layers, but the bottom we can find Linux kernel.

6
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:39,280
 So Android is built on top of Linux kernel.

7
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:43,800
 And above that there is the hull which is the hardware abstraction layer.

8
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,800
 And above that we have the Android runtime or the Dalvik virtual machine.

9
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:54,920
 At the same level we have the native libraries which are mostly returning C and C++.

10
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:59,600
 On top we have the Android framework which is returning Java.

11
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,960
 And at the top most we have the apps.

12
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:07,320
 So as an normal Android user will be mostly interacting with the apps.

13
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:13,080
 And the Android framework and the native libraries are basically a mediator between the apps

14
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,880
 and the hull or the next kernel.

15
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:20,520
 So we will discuss in detail about each of these layers in a later section.

16
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:25,040
 Next I would like to introduce you to source.android.com.

17
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:29,560
 So as an AOSP platform developer you should definitely look at this website.

18
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:32,400
 You get a lot of information about AOSP.

19
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,400
 You can find the English version here, socialtandro.com.

20
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,040
 And if you need a Chinese version you could use this one.

21
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:41,320
 There is also a link to the code provided by Google.

22
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:46,480
 So if you click on this you will take you to the code and here you can see the branch.

23
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:47,480
 It is the master now.

24
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:52,480
 So if you want to quickly reference refer the code of AOSP then you can use this link.

25
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:54,480
 CS.android.com.

26
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:57,800
 And here you can search for whatever modules you want.

27
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:02,440
 And similar to an editor you can use this but only for reading purpose.

28
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:08,640
 Search for system UI here then you can see the module and system UI related classes here.

29
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,880
 And here you have the options to change the branch.

30
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:14,880
 So if you are looking for some other branch you can of course use that branches here.

31
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:19,240
 And if you are looking for some commits that also you can see here.

32
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:24,320
 In source.android.com you can find a lot of information about AOSP.

33
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:30,880
 Under the docs you can see different documentations for getting started, security, core topics,

34
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:34,760
 compatibility, Android devices, reference.

35
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,400
 Under getting started there is the required information about how to download and build

36
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:42,480
 an AOSP project.

37
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:47,840
 Under you can find information about the platform structure, development environment setup,

38
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:53,920
 downloading and building the source, customizations and some information on how to contribute

39
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:58,360
 to Android open source project.

40
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:04,200
 Under security you can get an overview of Android security at different levels like in an

41
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:09,720
 Android application or even in the kernel level.

42
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:17,000
 It also details the security features provided by Android like SC Linux, key store, encryption,

43
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,040
 authentication and app signing.

44
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:27,360
 There is information on how to perform security test on your AOSP build and some best practices

45
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:29,880
 for security.

46
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:34,960
 Under core topics you can find a lot of information on different building blocks of Android.

47
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:40,400
 There is detailed explanation about the architecture and many essential modules in Android

48
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:44,640
 like audio, camera, media, etc.

49
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:50,640
 Under compatibility you can find the compatibility definition document and compatibility test

50
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:53,960
 your related information.

51
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:58,160
 These are important if you plan to build a pure Android device.

52
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:03,840
 If you want to keep Android logo on your device you should pass these CTS tests or

53
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:07,160
 compatibility test suit tests.

54
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:12,520
 You run the CTS test for your AOSP build and send it to Google for approval.

55
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:14,960
 But this is not mandatory.

56
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:20,840
 If you are simply building a device that does not need Android logo then this is optional.

57
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:26,680
 These test are quality gates to ensure that your build meets a basic quality standards

58
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:28,680
 set by Google.

59
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:35,920
 After Android devices we can find information specific to automotive, enterprise and TV.

60
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:40,640
 And finally there is a reference section under which you find more information about the

61
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:48,960
 hull, hardware abstraction layer, HIDL, hull interface definition language and trade federation

62
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:58,840
 which is basically a testing framework for continuous testing.

